[size=5][b]Crimson Desert Telemetry 2.2.1[/b][/size]

[b]All-around engine lights and physics visibility remain on by default. Version 2.2.1 improves visibility stability and ReShade 6.8 capture compatibility.[/b]

Telemetry now reads the game's light-source data [b]before the renderer's view-dependent filtering[/b]. Turning away no longer means losing those sources from your radar or telemetry stream. The game's own physics queries estimate whether geometry blocks the path from the camera to each nearby light.

[b]Supports Crimson Desert patch 2.03.02 on Steam for Windows.[/b]

[size=4][b]What's new in 2.2.1[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Visibility stability:[/b] continuous physics queries use the validated game-query context, preventing the synchronized false-clear flashes observed in 2.2.0.[/*]
[*][b]ReShade 6.8 compatibility:[/b] native capture recognizes the same D3D12 device through ReShade's proxy while still rejecting a different device.[/*]
[*][b]Capture startup:[/b] the probe command queue stays alive through the process lifetime, and preparation stages are logged for diagnosis.[/*]
[*][b]Optional lightshow bridge:[/b] a separate companion can temporarily modulate ManyLights RGB through a local bridge that disengages when its heartbeat expires. The companion is not included in this ZIP.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]All-around light features introduced in 2.2[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]All-around light capture:[/b] current engine lights remain available beyond the camera's view, including behind you.[/*]
[*][b]Physics-based occlusion:[/b] nine rays sample the path from the camera toward each light. Results are clear, blocked or unknown. Enabled by default.[/*]
[*][b]Fire groups decoded:[/b] particle groups become summed light contributions instead of dozens of separate particle records. One physical fire can still produce several contributions.[/*]
[*][b]Updated radar and markers:[/b] filled dots indicate renderer-selected lights; hollow rings indicate current sources the renderer did not select for this view. Hollow does not mean OFF.[/*]
[*][b]API schema 1.6:[/b] adds lights.upstream, renderer-selection information and physics visibility metadata while preserving the existing rendered-light stream.[/*]
[*][b]All-around smoothing:[/b] the separate grouped and smoothed stream now uses upstream light data when enabled.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]What you get[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Light data:[/b] world position, linear HDR RGB, derived luminance, and direction/cone angle for recognized spotlight contributions.[/*]
[*][b]Raw and smoothed streams:[/b] retain the original contributions or use a separate spatially grouped, configurable smoothing feed for your own applications.[/*]
[*][b]Player and camera:[/b] player position and root orientation, plus native render-camera position, orientation and projection parameters.[/*]
[*][b]Ambient/sky:[/b] global sky RGB, with optional camera-local sky visibility and a derived local ambient estimate when available.[/*]
[*][b]Two in-game views:[/b] fullscreen light markers with detail cards and a player-centered 3D radar with the camera frustum.[/*]
[*][b]Local HTTP/WebSocket APIs:[/b] use the data in overlays, tools, research or external lighting integrations.[/*]
[*][b]Single ASI:[/b] automatic host startup, configurable hotkeys, status notices and SDR/HDR10/scRGB UI.[/*]
[/list]

[url=https://youtu.be/eyRkkTXAU64]Watch the telemetry and HUD demonstration[/url]
The video shows an earlier version; all-around source capture and physics visibility were introduced in 2.2.0.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/v2.2.1/media/screenshot1.jpg[/img]

[size=4][b]How light visibility works[/b][/size]

The mod casts nine physics rays from the [b]camera position[/b] toward points around each light, including sources behind the camera.

[list]
[*][b]Clear:[/b] at least one sampled path is unobstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Blocked:[/b] all nine sampled paths are obstructed.[/*]
[*][b]Unknown:[/b] the measurement is missing, invalid, incomplete or older than 500 ms.[/*]
[/list]

This helps distinguish a wall from a lantern's metal cage: a cage bar can block the center ray while neighboring rays still pass through. Blocked markers are dimmed by default; press F11 to hide them. Unknown sources are not hidden.

[b]This is a sampled collision estimate, not optical transmission or a percentage of visible brightness.[/b] Narrow gaps can produce clear paths, while geometry without suitable collision may not block the queries.

The supplied radius is [b]100 game units around the player[/b], configurable from 1 to 500 through LightOverlay/Radius. Rays still originate at the camera. Up to 256 targets share a bounded query budget; crowded scenes can refresh individual lights less frequently.

Visibility metadata never removes API light records or changes their RGB. The smoothed colors are not automatically reduced by occlusion; consumers can use the supplied visibility metadata for their own filtering.

To disable physics visibility:

[code]
[SourceVisibility]
Enabled=0[/code]

[size=4][b]Install or upgrade[/b][/size]

Requires:
[list]
[*]Windows x64 and Crimson Desert patch [b]2.03.02[/b] on Steam.[/*]
[*]Microsoft [b].NET 8 ASP.NET Core Runtime (x64)[/b].[/*]
[*]One compatible x64 ASI loader installed for the game.[/*]
[/list]

[list]
[*]Close Crimson Desert before installing, updating or removing Telemetry.[/*]
[*]Back up your INI preferences. Disable/remove the previous Telemetry package and any separate legacy console ASI.[/*]
[*]Import and [b]activate/deploy[/b] the complete CrimsonDesertTelemetry-v2.2.1-ModManagers.zip in your mod manager. Importing alone is insufficient.[/*]
[*]Keep all six runtime files together beside the game EXE. Do not mix versions.[/*]
[*]Merge your preferences into the new INI, launch the game and load a save.[/*]
[/list]

[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.asi
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.ini
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg[/code]

[b]DMM upgrade note:[/b] Local tests with DMM 2.8.1 and 3.1.1 found that removal/upgrades can leave old Telemetry companions behind. With the game closed and the old package removed, check these four files before redeploying. Remove only stale Telemetry copies, not your ASI loader:

[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg[/code]

Keep the .cfg filenames unchanged. Renaming them to .json can make DMM interpret them as game patches.

Supplied defaults:

[code]
[Lights]
Upstream=1

[SourceVisibility]
Enabled=1

[LightOverlay]
Radius=100
HideOccluded=0[/code]

Restart the game after INI changes. Hot-unloading is unsupported.

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]F8:[/b] show/hide the corner HUD and 3D radar.[/*]
[*][b]F9:[/b] show/hide detailed diagnostics.[/*]
[*][b]F10:[/b] show/hide fullscreen light markers.[/*]
[*][b]F11:[/b] hide/show freshly classified blocked lights in both light views.[/*]
[/list]

All four shortcuts are configurable in the INI using decimal Windows virtual-key codes; 0 disables a shortcut. Hiding the HUD does not stop telemetry. Automatic hiding in every game menu is not implemented.

Brief success notices appear when data becomes ready. Startup and compatibility errors provide visible status information. Unsupported executables are rejected rather than using guessed addresses.

[size=4][b]HTTP and WebSocket[/b][/size]

The server binds to local IPv4 loopback only:

[code]
GET http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/snapshot
GET http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/health
GET http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/schema
WS  ws://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/stream

GET http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/lights/smoothed
WS  ws://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/lights/smoothed/stream

GET http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/ambient
GET http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/ambient/schema
WS  ws://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/ambient/stream[/code]

Snapshot schema 1.6 exposes [b]lights.upstream[/b] alongside the existing rendered records. Renderer selection and geometric visibility are separate: a source can be outside the view but still have a clear path to the camera.

The smoothed feed groups nearby contributions and applies a configurable exponential smoothing filter, defaulting to 200 ms. With upstream capture enabled, it reports unavailable when that source is missing rather than silently switching to view-filtered data. The HUD can fall back to rendered data and labels this [b]RENDERED ONLY[/b].

[url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/blob/main/docs/API.md]API reference[/url]
[url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/tree/main/examples]Client examples[/url]

The telemetry API does not switch game lights or control physical lamps. The optional lightshow bridge is separate and changes RGB only while its companion is active.

[size=4][b]Data limits and compatibility[/b][/size]

[b]All-around does not mean a permanent registry of every lamp in the world.[/b] The upstream stream covers supported light records the engine currently supplies before view selection. Streaming and earlier engine decisions still limit coverage. A missing record does not prove a lamp is OFF.

A physical lamp can have multiple contributions. Group positions can be derived centers, and proximity grouping is not proven object identity. Authored, rendered and upstream arrays overlap: do not add them together.

Special negative-RGB/view-space records are excluded from the upstream stream and counted in diagnostics. The existing rendered stream remains available separately.

Linear HDR RGB is renderer data, not lumens, 0–255 color or final screen pixels. Values can exceed 1.0. Tone mapping and physical-lamp conversion belong to the consuming application.

[b]Camera-local sky visibility and derived local ambient RGB remain optional.[/b] They can be unavailable for an entire session on patch 2.03.02 even while global sky RGB works. Check availability and freshness.

[b]No NVIDIA or DLSS requirement.[/b] Neither Nsight nor PIX is needed to run the mod. Capture and UI use DirectX 12. SDR, HDR10 and FP16 scRGB have automated test coverage; live HDR-display and AMD/Intel game testing remain unverified.

Technical compatibility identifiers: EXE 1.0.0.2976, Steam build 25477059. A future game update can require a mod update.

[size=4][b]Validation and support[/b][/size]

The 2.2.1 production package passed the managed Release suite, [b]37/37 native tests[/b] and package validation. The focused visibility correction was confirmed in game. The sporadic capture-start hang's root cause remains unproven.

[url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/blob/main/docs/releases/v2.2.1-validation.md]2.2.1 validation record[/url]

If a startup error or crash occurs, include the game crash record and these logs from beside the game EXE:

[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.bootstrap.log
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.host.log
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.native.log
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.overlay.log[/code]

The release retains the earlier host-startup and swapchain-lifetime fixes. WebView is not a runtime requirement. Historical GPU hangs were recorded, but their causes were not conclusively established; a successful test is not a promise of compatibility with every system.

